Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

A new thread, one that interests me a great deal. But first, this
inspiring message from Perry:

> > What is the status of the messages sent to the list while panix was
> > down? 
> 
> Myself, I hope they are gone. It might mean that we've broken a few
> silly conversational cycles, and might get some unusually high S to N
> for a while.
> 
> Perry


Indeed, the hiatus has been welcome.

I hope the topic I mention here is in fact a high S/N theme, even if
"predicting the stock market" or "beating the averages" turns out to
be impossible (never say impossible...).

Several things have come together to rekindle my old interest in this
topic:

* Foremost, a good friend of mine is running a "hedge fund" and I've
been researching these entities. (A "hedge fund" differs from a mutual
fund in several ways: can make much riskier investments, including
short positions, options, puts and calls, etc., is open only to
"qualified investors," and has a limited number of subscribers.)

I was skeptical of his reported annual rates of return (> 30% on
average, for the past N years), having read Burton Malkiel's "A Random
Walk Down Wall Street" at an impressionable age. But I got to thinking
about this problem, using stuff from the AIT VirtSem on
Chaitin/Kolmogorov information theory, and similar "machine learning"
ideas (as Derek Zahn, especially, was talking about last summer).

Still, my friend the hedge fund manager was formerly V.P. and Chief
Scientist of the Xerox Imaging division in Palo Alto, and is very
bright. (Perry may be justifiably skeptical that an "amateur" can beat
the rocket scientists on Wall Street...maybe I am too.)

As I may conceivably move some money into this hedge fund, I am
strongly motivated to once again look at this question of time series
analysis and whether any "system" (a mechanistic, computer-based
model) can actually outperform the market.

(Simple schemes involving "candlestick formations" and other such
naive witchcraft seem bogus, but some money managers have apparently
outperformed the market averages....this could be an artifact, as with
the "brilliant penny" scam, though. I'm reading Jack Schwager's
"Market Wizards," a series of detailed interviews with many Wall
Street hotshots and independent investors.)

* I should be receiving the new book "An Introduction to Kolmogorov
Complexity and Its Applications," by Ming Li and Paul Vitanyi, in a
few days. 546 pages of juicy stuff on string complexity and
predictability, compression, and portfolio analysis. (The price vector
of a stock resembles--and can be interpreted as--a string. Cover and
Thomas have some interesting things to say about this in "Elements of
Information Theory," about which I've talked several times.) 

If our AIT VirtSem were still going, this would be a book for us to
read. As it is, I'll study it and maybe summarize some interesting points.

* There are some loose links to crypto, of course, in the sense of
compressiblity, "cracking the code" of the market, predictablity,
entropy (randomness or order), etc.

* Having been doing a fair amount of trading lately (Qualcomm, Novell,
3D), Lotus, Apple, Intel, Compaq,...), my interest in formally
analyzing the market has been sharpened.

So, multiplying wealth by analysis seems pretty Extropian to me.

How about to you?

--Tim

