From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

   Some time ago, I thought I discovered a magic way to earn money
on options; there are arbitrage transactions (like butterfly spread)
that I could (and still can) find in practically every stock market
report that, surprisingly enough, bring you money when you make them...
and make some more money when you liquidate them! - no investment
required!
    Later I found that executing multiple transactions simultaneously
is not easy, and that broker commissions would eat a lion share of the
profit.
    There's still a chance that the scheme can work on an automated
system though.
    In a general description, the idea involves reconstructing
a probabilistic function of futures' prices (as expected by the
market) from option prices, and capitalize on the deviations of the
individual prices from this curve.  The nice thing about it is that
you don't have to know where the prices are going at all.
    I looked into some thick books on stock market logic.
They call the simplest second-order arbitrage transaction, the
butterfly spread, 'difficult' and 'sophisticated' and don't
mention any terms like 'second-order effects', 'probabilistic curves',
etc.  I understand why - this stuff is beyond the math education of
even a good professional broker (in my experience, at least).
    The 'math' models they use (like 'delta' factors, etc.) I find
rather amusing.
    However, if there are many enough good computer trading systems
out there, my arbitrage scheme won't work. Are there? I don't know...
