From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

Sasha writes:
>     In a general description, the idea involves reconstructing
> a probabilistic function of futures' prices (as expected by the
> market) from option prices, and capitalize on the deviations of the
> individual prices from this curve. 

Perhaps Sasha has reinvented the Black-Scholes formula:

call option value = S Norm(x) - K(1+r)^(-t) * Norm(x-var)

where x = ln(S/K(1+r)^(-t))/var + var/2)

and var = (expected standard deviation in S over period t)*sqrt(t)

S is the price of the underlying security, K is the option's
exercise price, Norm is the normal distribition function, r is
the risk-free rate of return, and t is time until maturity.  In 
other words, an option's current price should be a function of 
the expected volatility of the underlying security, square root of 
the time to maturity, option strike price, and current price of the 
underlying security, assuming prices movements are random and normally 
distributed.

One can make pure volatility plays by constructing butterfly spreads 
(to bet on lower volatility) or straddles (to bet on increased
volatility).   A straddle is a call and put on the same security,
same exercise price, same expiration date.  A butterfly consists
of buying 1 call option with an exercise price below the current
security price, selling 2 call options at the security price, and 
buying another call option above the security price.  This is
not arbitrage; these instruments do rely on predicting the future, 
albeit the variance in the price over time, not the price itself.
For example, if you have unique info that a previously conservative
company is spending most of its R&D budget on one very risky new 
product, but you have no idea whether that product will be a winner 
or loser, you can still make money by buying a straddle spread on the 
company.  To further hedge away risk unrelated to the accuracy of your
information, you can buy a corresponding volume of butterfly spreads 
on the company's competitors, assuming transaction costs are low enough.
You are then making a bet that the company's volatility will increase
relative to its competitors, so if the volatility of the entire
industry increases or decreases for some reason, it doesn't matter.

To take advantage of Black-Scholes mispricings, 
an arbitrage between a security and its options
can be constructed by selling short the security and buying a call
option on it, or buying the security and a put option for it.
Both are equivalent to the risk-free asset + mispricing between
the security and its option, so if you have found a mispricing
this locks in a risk-free profit.  In this case no prediction
of the future is involved, other than the prediction implicit
in the assumption that your model for calculating the mispricing
is accurate (eg Black-Scholes arbitragers typically use a 
weighted average of past volatility, and assume future volatility
will be the same).

There are many slightly different and more accurate elaborations 
of Black-Scholes that run full-tilt in roomfulls of computers on Wall 
Street, making split-second measurements of stock/option spreads 
across world markets and adjusting accordingly.  If
new information gets put in the market by somebody trading in the
option but not the stock, or vice versa, these arbitrage 
machines jump right on it and correct the mispricing.  The
arbitrage companies also get special bulk rates on trades, so 
their transaction costs are far lower than the individual trader's,
so they can make money on much narrower spreads.

In the mid-80s there was consistent mispricing on the Nikkei 
index options and American Black-Scholes arbs made $bundles.  That
was one of the last big Black-Scholes arb opportunities to close.  
The big task now is making sure the software includes all the 
elaborations and exceptions to Black-Scholes, for example during 
chaotic moments when major volatility-changing news, eg takeover
rumors, enters the market.

