Subject: scams

Players: p * n gamblers.

Get p * n people to call you the evening before, or morning of, a
horse race with n runners, to receive "information".  You have
p people allocated to each horse in the race, each agreeing to pay
you the odds to, say, $50 if "the" horse wins.  When a horse wins,
you receive the odds * p * $50.  The handwritten letters of thanks
for the "successful information" that people send in with the money
are then photocopied and used as "evidence" of the "quality of the
information" in subsequent mailshots.  Where n is the average field
size in a number of races, every 1 in n ^ m people is lucky enough
to start off with m consecutive wins, so their letters would be
particularly useful in building up new clientele.

