Agreed.  I interpreted this as "OR it doesn't happen", this of course is
the Newtonian mechanics strict-determinist view which I realise you agree
is obsolete.  What you say about causality is an uncontroversial axiom,
but events such as human action are partly caused by mental states, and
partly by external stimuli.  Sometimes mental states are themselves
caused by external stimuli, but we only need some randomness in meme
creation together with memetic selection by the individual in order to
decouple individual from environmental causation. 

Let's say a wasp flies in through the window.  Two minutes later it's
lying squashed outside on the ground, having come between a rolled-up
newspaper and a hard object.  But a week later, another wasp flies in,
and is allowed to live.  Although the same stimulus showed up, my
internal mental state was not the same, and I took a different course
of action.  Maybe I didn't want to get the newspaper dirty.  Or perhaps
I'd decided that since the wasp season was almost over by now, they
weren't much of a nuisance and I could justifiably ignore them.  It
might even be that I felt sorry for them, or perhaps I just couldn't
be bothered to deal with it.  But we're free to change our mental
states, which we sometimes do rationally, sometimes irrationally and
sometimes arationally.   

In the first example, the action was that the wasp got swatted.  But
did it happen because the wasp flew in, or because I decided to 'get'
it?  It couldn't have been just because it flew in, because the next
one flew out unmolested.  Just like software is like an adjective
describing how a computer operates, each of us has a memecomplex of
values, beliefs, memories, intentions and dreams.  Each individual's
memecomplex determines what s/he does, together with external influences.
But it's not difficult to imagine that these memecomplexes are not
totally determined by environment (or even genetically!), and also the 
individual has some say in their evolution.

In biological/genetic evolution, random mutations occur in genes which
are then selected for if they confer a relevant advantage.  The
mutations, by the way, can occur because of quantum indeterminacy such
as radiation.  But randomness and selection ends up producing something
far greater than the original primordial soup.  No external Being was
necessary to create creatures that could marvel at the universe's
complexity; randomness played an important part in their formation, and
from randomness arises purpose.  It's likely that our memecomplexes are
awash with a sea of spontaneously created memes, and a selection procedure
operates on these memetic mutations according to the current beliefs and
values held by the individual.  Some new ideas could arise and go on to
have varying impacts on the individual's future behaviour -- maybe very
slight, or possibly quite profound.  But the slightest influence is
enough to break the causal chain.  It's these emergent spontaneous orders
that are the interesting thing, and I suspect an individual's memetic
development is rather Lamarckian. 

But let's see what happens if the causal chain is not broken.  What if
everything we do is determined by the environment?  This in itself would
be rather an achievement!  I think it was you who said something like,
"However could you have *unfree* will?"  But under the causality axiom,
caused events are unfree and random events are free, so we've either got
"will" or "freedom" and not the two together.

In this case we've got two powers:  "will" and "free will".  When we
"will" something this is your example of rational thinking, determined
by brain state and environment.  We can also choose to make random
arational decisions if we want, and this is "free will".  Our brain state
is determined partly by environment and rational thought, and partly by
other kinds of thought.  Looking at all the points in our past, sometimes
we made mistakes, other times we truly made the best choice.  So rational
thought should tend to produce the best decisions, with other choices being
of a more chancy nature.  A sequence of choices might go like this:
...1,6,3,2,8,1,3,2,1,4,1,3,2,1,2....  where a '1' was a consequence of
rational thought, the best choice at the time.  And it's not always
possible to determine where each decision comes on the ranking order.

But if every single decision that we made was caused by environment
rather than by us, that would imply that it was really Hobson's choice;
the only possible choice was the 'best' choice every time, and we were
not free to choose second best.  This would seem to be something worth
striving for.  If you never made a mistake, but always made the very
best decision according to your current values and memecomplex,
sacrificing metaphysical freedom for this would be a good trade.  (And
you only need to be able to occasionally make arational choices, like
where each possible branch has equivalent values, to get all the
advantages _and_ know that you're not an automaton!)

>All rational thought is deterministic ,the very word "reason"
