I recently got the Paul Churchland book *Matter and Consciousness*,
and these are a couple of the points he makes about parallel systems
(in addition to speed).  (The book is in the Extropians reading list,
so it must even be Extropianly Correct [E.C.]!)  

In my listing of various brain/mind positions, I mentioned emergent
interactionism as my favourite; I've since reread a part of the
Popper/Eccles book *The Self and its Brain* that I hadn't read for ages
and realised how far apart the two of them are from my position on
important issues.  Karl Popper is a deathist, claiming that death is a
"positively valuable element in life".  John Eccles is a substance
dualist, believing that the mind or "soul" can have an independent
existence; an idea that I rejected at the age of six or seven.  

But if something is able to exert an influence, it can be considered 
to exist in some sense.  We could have a material object e.g. a computer
whose operation was partly dependent on software (which has a physical
embodiment but could also be considered as existing in some other domain)
and partly on the laws of logic and mathematics (which certainly have an
existence but not a material one).  A human's behaviour would be in
accordance with the laws of logic and mathematics, and the memecomplex.
Maybe the memecomplex (including memories) is sufficient to totally
define identity and hence the Self, so if the memecomplex has control,
the Self has control. 

One mind-body position that I omitted was functionalism, which may be
more to my liking.  I particularly liked Paul Churchland's point that
the functionalists believe a science of conscious intelligence can
claim a discontinuous autonomy from established natural sciences such
as physics and biology on the grounds of a unique feature.  So it
doesn't look too reductionist to me.  

As for hardware requirements, how about if structures in a brain
represent particular memes and have meaning because of their interwiring
and hence associations with other memes?  So on their own they have no
absolute meaning and are just a bunch of neurons; the meaning only becomes
apparent relative to other memes.  'Red' by itself is just a position in
color qualia space, but its association with 'blood' and 'bacon' give it
meaning, whose associations give them meaning in turn ad infinitum.  (Plus
you have visual and verbal representation, and perhaps distant memories of
when the concept was first learned.)  A serial machine where 'red' was
just a memory location or symbol would have to have the associated memes
'fired' in order to understand what it was processing, and this firing
would have to be unpredictable to prevent the machine from feeling that
it was in a dream, or a parallelists' world!

